not already covered by non-removable ones. That's a typical pruning.\n\nThus the algorithm:\n- Build a mapping from id to node.\n- Build children mapping.\n- Determine set of required_symbols.\n- Determine set of non_removable node IDs (from removable flag).\n- Determine which required symbols are already satisfied by non_removable nodes: for each required symbol, if there exists a non_removable node with that symbol, mark that symbol as satisfied.\n- For each remaining unsatisfied required symbol, we need to include at least one node with that symbol. We need to choose which node(s) to include. To minimize tree size, we would include one such node and all its ancestors (which may be removable or not). But we must also ensure that we do not include more than necessary. Possibly we need to include all ancestors of that node (since parent must be kept). So we add that node and its path to root.\n- After that, we have a set of kept nodes. However, we also must consider that some non_removable nodes might be descendants of removable nodes that we removed? But non_removable nodes must be kept regardless. If a non_removable node's parent is removable and we removed it, that would break the closure property because parent must also be kept. So we cannot remove a node that is an ancestor of any non_removable node. Therefore, all ancestors of non_removable nodes must be kept. Since the root is non_removable, that's fine. But for non_removable nodes at depth >1, their parent must be kept, even if removable. So we must include all ancestors of all non_removable nodes. That means we cannot remove any node that is on the path from root to a non_removable node. In the fixture, n1 (non_removable) is child of root, so root is kept. n3 (non_removable) is child of root. n5 (non_removable) is child of n3. So n3 must be kept, and root must be kept. n5's parent is n3, already kept. So no additional ancestors needed for non_removable. But n2 is removable and is not ancestor of any non_removable, so can be removed. n4 is child of n2, removable, not ancestor of non_removable, so can be removed. n6 is child of n1, removable, n1 is non_removable, so n6 is descendant of non_removable, but n6 is not ancestor of any non_removable, so can be removed? However, if we remove n6, its parent n1 is kept, but n6 itself is removable and not required, so it's okay to remove it (we just delete it, no issue). So n6 can be removed.\n\nThus the kept set becomes: root, n1, n3, n5. So output: list of node IDs.\n\nBut what about replacement candidates? Maybe we also need to consider if we can replace a node with its children (i.e., promote children) to remove the node? That would be a transformation that changes depth. But the problem statement mentions \"deletion and replacement candidates\". Replacement could mean replacing a node with one of its children (i.e., removing the node and attaching its children to its parent). This is typical in tree reduction. But then the tree structure changes. However, the input nodes have fixed ids and parent references; we cannot modify them. The output is likely a new list of nodes that represent the reduced tree. Possibly we need to output a list of nodes that are kept, with possibly modified parent references if we do replacements? The fixture does not specify output format.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll assume the simplest interpretation: we need to return a list of node IDs that remain after pruning removable nodes that are not needed to satisfy required symbols, while preserving all non-removable nodes and their ancestors. This yields a well-defined output for each case.\n\nBut the \"solve(case)\" must return a JSON-serializable object. So we can return a dictionary like {\"kept\": [\"root\", \"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\"]} or just the list. Possibly the expected output is the reduced tree nodes (like the node objects that are kept). But the function signature says \"solve(case)\" where case is a dictionary with payload. Usually, in such