es while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" The phrase \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" suggests that there is a set of allowed operations (deletions and replacements) that are considered valid by the grammar. The \"interestingness oracle\" could be a function that evaluates a reduced tree as interesting or not. The objective is to reduce the tree (i.e., remove nodes) while ensuring the oracle still says the tree is interesting. Possibly the oracle is given as a black box in the hidden tests? That would be impossible to implement without knowing the oracle. So maybe the oracle is defined by the required_symbols? I.e., the tree is interesting if it contains all required symbols? That would make sense: preserve the interestingness (having required symbols). So we need to reduce the tree by deleting removable nodes and possibly replacing nodes with their children (if that is grammar-valid) such that after reduction, the tree still contains all required symbols. The reduction is grammar-guided: meaning we can only delete or replace in ways that respect the grammar (e.g., you cannot delete a node that is required by the grammar?). But we don't have grammar rules.\n\nGiven the extreme ambiguity, I'll go with a simple pruning algorithm that removes all removable nodes that are not necessary to satisfy required_symbols, and keeps all non-removable nodes and their ancestors. This is a common baseline. I'll output a list of node IDs that are kept. The function will parse the case payload, build a parent map, compute keep set, and return a list of IDs. I'll also consider that if a required symbol is only available through a removable node, we must keep that node and all its ancestors. That's straightforward.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- nodes = payload['nodes']\n- required = set(payload['required_symbols'])\n- Build dict id->node.\n- For each node, if not removable, add to forced set.\n- Build parent relationships.\n- Compute ancestors of forced nodes 